","resolve_class routes through _resolve_target (provider qualification/inference, endpoint handling) which a bespoke TOML read of only the `model` field would bypass"],"finding":"resolve_pinned_model repackages the existing model-class resolution seam: it proposes a bespoke read of rebar.toml's [llm.model_classes] `model` field and a hand-rolled fallback drop, but rebar.llm.model_classes already exposes load_class_slots() + resolve_class(name, slots) for a class's primary provider:model target and resolve_fallback_chain(name, slots) for its fallback candidates. The plan does not consider building on them.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"fd6befd55f40b7df0","impact":1.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Approach → 'Pinned targets': resolve_pinned_model","norm_id":"nc86f29737d23ebf7","priority":0.8889,"reason":"high-priority+criterion-opted-in","recalled":false,"scenarios":["A frontier class configured with a bare/provider-inferred model id (no explicit provider:) — resolve_class qualifies it, a raw `model` field read would not, so the startswith('bedrock:') guard could misjudge parity","A malformed pyproject/rebar.toml — load_class_slots degrades to documented defaults; a bespoke read may raise or read stale values, drifting from every other class consumer"],"severity":"critical","suggested_fix":"Have resolve_pinned_model call load_class_slots() then resolve_class(PASS_MODEL_CLASS[pass_name], slots) to get the primary provider:model id (applying the startswith('bedrock:') refusal to that resolved id), and rely on resolve_fallback_chain being simply ignored to compose the homogeneous single-provider chain — rather than re-parsing the TOML table and the `fallback` key by hand.","tier":"LLM","validity":0.8889,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"na","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"na","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"yes","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_ent